---
// Contact (/contact/) — specs/pages/contact.md. The minimal archetype: a
// short editorial column, no sidebar, no card grid, no index idiom.
//
// The page is thin by nature — roughly fifty words and three links is its
// honest maximum (§1, §2.1). Anything added here to make it look less empty
// is the exact failure this route was warned about, and §5 records what
// happens then: the content folds into /about/ and the route retires.
//
// No dynamic layer, permanently. No form (ADR 0020 authorizes no endpoint),
// no booking embed, no availability badge, no response-time promise (§2.2,
// §6). Every link is a plain anchor that works with scripts disabled.
//
// The address is a plain mailto: with its cost accepted — it will be scraped
// (§2.3). Obfuscating it with JavaScript would make the site's most basic
// affordance depend on a script and would not defeat modern harvesting.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { email, emailAddress, github, linkedin } from '../lib/links';
// The page header, taken from the index family rather than restated here. The
// rule this file wrote by hand was byte-identical to IndexHeader.astro's —
// 32px of padding under the title, a 1px --border rule, and the same five
// type declarations and colour on the h1, value for value. Two rules that
// merely resembled each other would differ somewhere; these did not differ
// anywhere, which is evidence both were transcribed from one prototype device
// rather than authored twice.
//
// So this is one device being reused, not the minimal archetype quietly
// becoming an index. Nothing else in that family comes with it: no entry
// list, no empty state, no card grid. If this page's header ever has to
// differ, it stops calling this component and says why.
import IndexHeader from '../components/IndexHeader.astro';
// The label/value row, shared with /about/ and /colophon/. The minimal
// archetype takes the register but not the deep-dive shell: no article.css,
// no sidebar, no prose components — there is no prose to compose here. The
// section device is not taken either: this page has one list and no headings
// to file it under, so Section.astro is absent rather than empty.
import RefRows from '../components/RefRows.astro';
import RefRow from '../components/RefRow.astro';

// Shown as what they are, not as icons (§2, section 4). The label is the
// account name a visitor would recognize; the link text is the destination.
const links = [
  { label: 'email', href: email, text: emailAddress, external: false },
  { label: 'github', href: github, text: 't-bendet', external: true },
  { label: 'linkedin', href: linkedin, text: 'tal-bendet', external: true },
];
---

<Base
  title="contact · T://bendet"
  description="How to reach Tal Bendet: email, GitHub, LinkedIn."
  section="contact"
>
  <IndexHeader title="contact" />

  {/* The openness statement (§2, section 3) — the sentence that earns this
      route existing. No response-time promise, no availability badge: when
      availability changes, this text changes. No Hebrew paragraph here; that
      audience is addressed natively in Hebrew on /he/writing/ (§2.4).

      Its 52ch measure is arbitrary because it is not a scale value: it is a
      lede measure, set narrower than the 64ch the index descriptions use and
      the 68ch of an article body, because two sentences under a title are
      read at a glance rather than down a column. */}
  <p class="mbs-8 text-size-lede leading-tight text-text max-inline-[52ch]">
    I am open to frontend and full-stack work in Israel. I also take translation requests:
    if an English article helped you and belongs in Hebrew, send it.
  </p>

  {/* The row's value slot is a link here rather than prose — mono, because it
      is an address, and isolated because an address is an LTR run wherever it
      sits. `[direction:ltr]` and `[unicode-bidi:isolate]` are arbitrary
      PROPERTIES: neither property has a utility in Tailwind 4.3.3, verified
      against the installed package, and SiteHeader.astro writes the same pair
      around the mark for the same reason.

      The hover rule this replaced was `text-decoration: underline`, and that
      is a SHORTHAND — it also put text-decoration-color back to currentcolor
      and text-decoration-thickness back to auto, over the base `a` rule's 1px
      --border-strong in global.css. The `underline` utility is a longhand and
      sets the line alone, so `decoration-current` and `decoration-auto` are
      that reset written back by hand. They are not garnish: dropping them is
      the defect that already shipped once in this migration and cost 61
      pixels on /colophon/ (specs/notes-tailwind-verdict.md §4.4, failure 1).

      `[&:hover]:` and never `hover:`. Tailwind v4 wraps `hover:` in
      `@media (hover: hover)`, which would delete the state on every
      coarse-pointer device — and no screenshot reports that. */}
  <RefRows class="mbs-10">
    {
      links.map((link) => (
        <RefRow>
          <span class="font-mono text-size-label tracking-label uppercase text-muted">
            {link.label}
          </span>
          <a
            class="font-mono text-size-meta text-text-strong no-underline [direction:ltr] [unicode-bidi:isolate] [&:hover]:underline [&:hover]:decoration-current [&:hover]:decoration-auto [&:hover]:underline-offset-[0.3em]"
            href={link.href}
            rel={link.external ? 'noopener' : undefined}
          >
            {link.text}
          </a>
        </RefRow>
      ))
    }
  </RefRows>
</Base>
